   140	### 2.3 Trusted-computing-base identity
   141	
   142	```json
   143	{
   144	  "record_version": "hardened-tcb-v1",
   145	  "hardened_profile": "hardened-profile-v1",
   146	  "execution_policy": "hardened-worker-v1",
   147	  "platform": "macos",
   148	  "enforcement_backend": "macos-sandbox-v1",
   149	  "supervisor_sha256": "sha256:...",
   150	  "worker_sha256": "sha256:...",
   151	  "toolchain": {"algorithm": "curator-go-toolchain-v1", "content_sha256": "sha256:..."}
   152	}
   153	```
   154	
   155	The TCB record names every mutable component whose compromise defeats the
   156	guarantees: the supervisor bytes, the worker bytes, the enforcement backend, and
   157	the fingerprinted toolchain. It is a closed record.
   158	
   159	An implementation that starts the supervisor or the worker through a mutable
   160	interpreter, an installed package tree, a script, or any other component it does
   161	not fingerprint MUST add those components to this record and MUST say so; it
   162	MUST NOT report a TCB record that is narrower than what it actually trusts.
   163	
   164	`platform` MUST be the hardened platform name of the host the operation runs
   165	on, and it MUST agree with the native target of the build input: `darwin` maps
   166	to `macos`, `linux` to `linux`, and `windows` to `windows`. `toolchain` MUST be
   167	the same `curator-go-toolchain-v1` identity the build input carries.
   168	
   169	#### `curator-hardened-tcb-v1`
   170	
   171	The record has one digest, and it is what the hashed build input carries.
   172	Initialize SHA-256 with exact ASCII `curator-hardened-tcb-v1` followed by
   173	`0x00`, then append `uint64be(length)` and the exact `CCJ-1` canonical bytes of
   174	the record, using the canonicalization `protocol/registry.md` defines and
   175	`protocol/core.md` section 9 already uses for build inputs and receipts. Prefix
   176	the lowercase digest with `sha256:`.
   177	
   178	The algorithm is domain-separated and length-framed for the same reason
   179	`curator-build-source-v1` is: the digest MUST NOT be confusable with a cache
   180	key, a receipt hash, or any other SHA-256 taken over the same canonical bytes.
   181	
   182	Consequently the TCB binds cache reuse, receipt bytes, `receipt_sha256`, the
   183	install marker, and the conformance claim. Section 8 states each binding and
   184	section 8.3 states why the resulting per-host key divergence is intended.
   185	
