   821	| Input | Distinguished by | Valid hardened input |
   822	|---|---|---|
   823	| pre-revision rc.4 candidate | no execution policy at all | no |
   824	| portable | `manager-worker-v1` | no |
   825	| rc.5 reserved policy slot only | `hardened-worker-v1` and nothing else | no |
   826	| hardened | profile identity and TCB digest A | yes |
   827	| hardened, after a manager update | profile identity and TCB digest B | yes |
   828	
   829	**Cache reuse is bound to the profile identity and to the concrete trusted
   830	computing base.** On lookup a manager recomputes the key from the profile
   831	identity of this revision and the TCB record it verified in
   832	`tcb-identity-verification`. An entry produced under another execution policy,
   833	another profile revision, or another trusted computing base therefore has a
   834	different key and is structurally a miss; there is nothing to relabel.
   835	
   836	In addition, a manager MUST treat as a miss any entry whose receipt does not
   837	carry a `hardened-tcb-v1` record byte-identical to the one it verified for this
   838	operation, and MUST NOT adopt, upgrade, downgrade, re-label, or re-sign such an
   839	entry in place. A manager that implements both profiles MUST keep their entries
   840	logically disjoint and MUST revalidate the protected-cache boundary of
   841	`protocol/core.md` section 9.3 for each profile independently.
   842	
   843	The hardened capability-evidence record does not participate in the cache key
   844	or in the hit comparison.
   845	
   846	### 8.2 Receipts
   847	
   848	A hardened local `go-v1` build writes **build receipt schema 3**. A hardened
   849	external `go-repository-v1` build writes **build receipt schema 4**. Both keep
   850	the receipt structure of `protocol/core.md` sections 9.1 and 9.2 — schema
   851	version, logical cache key, complete build input, and artifact path, SHA-256,
   852	and byte length — and both add one member:
   853	
   854	```json
   855	{"tcb": {"record_version": "hardened-tcb-v1", "...": "..."}}
   856	```
   857	
   858	A hardened receipt therefore binds all three identities: the execution policy
   859	and the profile identity inside the input, the TCB as the complete record plus
   860	its digest inside the input. A reader MUST reject a receipt whose
   861	`input.hardened.tcb.content_sha256` is not the `curator-hardened-tcb-v1` digest
   862	of its own `tcb` record, and MUST reject a receipt whose `cache_key` is not the
   863	digest of its own input.
   864	
   865	Receipt schemas 1 and 2 remain exactly as candidate `1.0.0-rc.5` defines them
   866	and MUST NOT be widened. A reader that does not implement the hardened profile
   867	MUST reject receipt schema 3 and 4 as unsupported receipt identities under
   868	`protocol/core.md` section 9.3; it MUST NOT parse them leniently, convert them,
   869	or treat them as schema 1 or 2.
   870	
   871	`receipt_sha256` is computed exactly as in `protocol/core.md` section 9.1 over
   872	the exact stored canonical bytes, so it covers the TCB record as well.
   873	
   874	The hardened capability-evidence record MUST NOT appear in a receipt. It
   875	reports what one operation observed, not what the artifact was produced under.
   876	
   877	### 8.3 Why the trusted computing base is hashed
   878	
   879	Hashing the TCB digest makes one source, toolchain, and contract produce a
   880	different key on each trusted computing base, and therefore after a manager
   881	update that changes the supervisor or worker bytes. That divergence is intended
   882	and is the point of the binding.
   883	
   884	An artifact carries exactly the guarantees of the trusted computing base that
   885	produced it. If a supervisor is updated — including to fix a containment defect
   886	— then reusing an artifact built under the previous supervisor would silently
   887	attribute this revision's guarantees to a build that never ran under them. The
   888	fail-closed answer is the same one section 7 gives everywhere else: rebuild
   889	rather than assume. A hardened cache miss costs a compile; a wrong attribution
   890	costs the guarantee.
   891	
   892	This is not the case `decisions/0006` rejected. That decision kept *per-operation
   893	capability evidence* out of portable cache identity, because evidence is an
   894	observation and a reader must not read a cache key as capability proof. The
   895	same rule still holds here: section 6.4's record stays result-only. What is
   896	hashed is not evidence but identity — which trusted code, which backend, which
   897	toolchain — which a reader can verify independently and which does not vary
   898	within one operation.
   899	
   900	The residual risk in the other direction — a host that silently loses a
   901	capability between operations — is answered by section 7, which re-probes and
   902	re-establishes every guarantee on every operation.
